A <functor> $U:\mathcal C\to\mathbf{Set}$ is a <representable functor> if some object $R$ admits a <natural isomorphism> $\mathcal C(R,-)\cong U$. Here the representable is covariant.

For the identity <functor> on the <category of sets>, choose the singleton $1=\{*\}$. The evaluation maps
$$
\mathbf{Set}(1,X)\longrightarrow X,\qquad f\longmapsto f(*)
$$
are <bijections> with inverse $x\mapsto(*\mapsto x)$. For $u:X\to Y$, evaluation of $uf$ is $u(f(*))$, proving naturality. Hence **the identity functor on sets is represented by a singleton**.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For a set $S$, let $\iota_s:1\to S$ select $s$. Given any family $f_s:1\to X$, define $f:S\to X$ by $f(s)=f_s(*)$. Then $f\iota_s=f_s$, and these equations determine every value of $f$, so it is unique.

This is precisely the universal property of a <coproduct in a category>, giving
$$
\boxed{S\cong\coprod_{s\in S}1.}
$$
The empty set gives the empty <coproduct in a category>, namely the <initial object> of the <category of sets>.

